# WordPress.org readme.txt

Before editing `readme.txt` or cutting a WordPress.org release, check every item. A failing check blocks the release.

## Limits

- **Short description** (the single line under the header, before `== Description ==`): **150 characters maximum**. Count the characters. Plain text only: no HTML and no Markdown. If it is 151 or longer, shorten it before release. WordPress.org truncates the public plugin card and warns the author.
- **Upgrade Notice**: each `== Upgrade Notice ==` version note is **300 characters maximum**.
- **File size**: keep `readme.txt` at or under **10KB**. A larger file can make the WordPress.org parser time out. The `== Description ==` section has no character limit and may use Markdown, but the whole file still counts. When the file is already over 10KB, do not add more changelog history to it: keep the current release in `== Changelog ==`, move older entries to `changelog.txt`, and leave a one-line pointer. Do not delete Description or External Services text just to hit 10KB unless Randy asks; report the byte size instead.

## Header

```
=== Plugin Name ===
== Description ==
= 1.0.0 =
```

- Title uses `=== ===`. Tabs use `== ==`. Changelog and FAQ subsections use `= =`.
- `Contributors:` comma-separated, case-sensitive WordPress.org usernames. Include `brelandr`.
- `Tags:` **5 maximum**, comma-separated. No competitor plugin names. No tags nobody would search for.
- `Tested up to`, `Requires at least`, and `Requires PHP` are numbers only (`7.1`, `7.4`), never `WP 7.1` or `PHP 7.4`. `Tested up to` is the major version (`7.1`).
- `Stable tag` is numbers and periods only (`8.5.6`). It must match the `Version:` header and `WGRSVP_VERSION`. Never `trunk` and never a `v` prefix.
- `License:` `GPLv2 or later`.
- Plugin name in `=== ===` must match the main plugin file `Plugin Name:` header.
